{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788302107,"nonce":"Q0UpFN/XzbjsAVFKMQAXaavkCrg=","title":"advance {until:{condition}} with an already-true predicate and no timeout runs UNBOUNDED — 187,541 ticks, stopped only by the casualty halt","message":"Found on a live bench in session 21, and it is the sharpest unattended-run hazard I have seen in this project. The command and its own result envelope are the whole report.\n\n## What was sent\n\n    {\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"until\":{\"condition\":{\"path\":\"time.tick\",\"op\":\"\u003e=\",\"value\":949}}}}\n\nNo `timeout_ticks`. The map's tick at arm time was **exactly 949**, so the predicate was TRUE the moment it was armed.\n\n## What happened\n\n    ok: true\n    reason: \"casualty\"\n    ticks_elapsed: 187541\n    tick: 188490\n    journal_unread: 47\n\n**It ran for 187,541 ticks — a bit over three in-game days — and the only thing that stopped it was the own-faction casualty halt that shipped THIS SESSION under `722c951`.** Without that halt it would have run until the process died.\n\nThe caller was an acceptance suite whose own `send()` timed out at 240s and reported a FAIL; every command after it came back `code: \"busy\"`, and the suite exited 2 on a fixture precondition, **leaving the advance running**. So a single malformed predicate produced: one wrong FAIL, two null-key FAILs downstream of it, a poisoned run for every later command, and a game left burning in-game days with nobody driving.\n\n## Why it happens, and why it is nobody's bug yet\n\nSession 19's decision (DESIGN, 2026-09-01) is that **`condition` requires an EDGE by default** — a predicate true at arm time does not halt immediately; it waits for a false→true transition. That decision is right and I am not disputing it.\n\nBut `time.tick \u003e= 949` is **monotonically true forever once true**. There is no false→true transition remaining, so under edge semantics the halt can never fire. Combine that with **no default `timeout_ticks`** and the advance is unbounded by construction.\n\n`accept/fc287ba-until-state.py` phase 3 does claim to prove the edge \"both ways on a predicate that is true the moment it is armed\" — so this case WAS tested. It passed because that suite supplied a timeout. The hole is only visible when the timeout is omitted, which is exactly what a hand-written or model-written call does.\n\n## Why this matters more than an ordinary refusal bug\n\n`664e9b9` is a **ten-day unattended run**. The whole point of `advance {until:{condition}}` (spec 1.6) is that the agent stops guessing tick counts and says \"advance until it's done\". This defect means a predicate that is already satisfied — the most natural mistake for a caller to make, and one a model will make constantly, since \"advance until the tick is at least N\" reads perfectly sensible — silently converts \"wait for a thing\" into \"burn the rest of the run\".\n\nIt is also a **liveness hazard on top of `65e7cf9`**: that issue is about a dead client leaving the game running. This is the same outcome from a live client that merely asked the wrong question.\n\n## Scope\n\nThree candidate fixes; the first is the one I would take, and they compose:\n\n1. **REFUSE AT ARM TIME when the predicate is already true and no `timeout_ticks` was given.** Session 19 already established the precedent that a path which does not resolve is a refusal at arm time; this is the same class — an arm-time check that the requested halt is reachable. The refusal must say the predicate is already true and name the edge rule, because the caller's mental model is \"it's true, so stop immediately\" and the answer is \"it's true, so I will never stop\".\n2. **Default `timeout_ticks` to something finite** rather than unbounded. An `until` with no bound is a request nobody should be able to make by omission. Pick the number and state the reasoning.\n3. Consider publishing, at arm time, whether the predicate was true when armed — `armed_true: bool` — so a caller can tell \"waited and it happened\" from \"was already true and I waited for a re-entry\".\n\nRead `Source/AutoRimmer/TimeDriver.cs` and `Source/AutoRimmer/StateWatch.cs`. Note the interaction with `722c951`: the casualty halt and the bleed-deadline refusal are now the only backstops on an unbounded advance, and they should not be load-bearing for this.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `advance {until:{condition}}` with a predicate that is TRUE at arm time and no `timeout_ticks` is REFUSED at arm time, and the refusal says the predicate is already true and why that means it will never fire.\n- The same call WITH a `timeout_ticks` still behaves as session 19 specified: it does not halt on the already-true predicate, and it stops at the timeout. Proven, not asserted — this is the branch that must not regress.\n- A predicate that is FALSE at arm time and becomes true still halts on the edge, unchanged.\n- An `until` advance with no `timeout_ticks` and a satisfiable predicate is bounded by whatever default item 2 chooses, and the envelope names that default and that it was applied rather than requested.\n- Proven on a monotonic predicate specifically (`time.tick \u003e= \u003cnow\u003e`), because that is the shape that cannot ever produce a second edge.","files":null}]}